"explanation": "A Selvar contraction first asks which edges qualify, using three structural tests on the input graph. The edge must not be a bridge, so removing it keeps the graph connected. It must lie on at least one cycle, which makes the bridge test redundant for connected graphs. It must also lie on no induced odd cycle, meaning no chordless cycle of odd length passes through it. If any edge meets all three tests, one such edge is contracted once. Self-loops are deleted, parallel edges are merged, and nothing is rechecked afterward. Trees and paths fail because every edge is a bridge. An isolated chordless triangle, pentagon or other odd hole fails because every edge lies on that induced odd cycle. A chordless even cycle such as C4 passes, because its only cycle is even and its edges are not bridges.",
